title: SleepyRunnerP on Hardware

files:
  - em.prog:em.benchmark.coremark/SleepyRunnerP.em.ts
  - .assets/img-101-01-01.png
  - .assets/img-102-08-01.png


steps:
  - cmds:
      - view 2
    text: |
      {[ht,$start]&thinsp;Signal Capture on Real Hardware}

      Until now, we've used **PulseView** to view sig&shy;nals captured by the **Wokwi LOGIC** block in the **Pi Pico** simulator.

      <div class="em-info">

      The last tour {[tr,102/07/05]} showed a **PulseView** capture of {[cu]SleepyRunnerP} &ndash; more compelling than {[cu]BlinkerP}.

      </div>
      
      In fact, you can *already* execute {[cu]SleepyRunnerP} on *any* of the boards presented in an earlier tour {[tr,102/06/02]}.

      But without **PulseView**, you'll miss out on lots of details&thinsp;&hellip;

  - cmds:
      - view 3
    text: |
      {[ht,monitor_heart]&thinsp;A Real Hardware Logic Analyzer}

      Good news&thinsp;&hellip;&thinsp; You can purchase a real logic analyzer for less than many of these inexpensive development boards.

      <div class="em-info">

      [Sparkfun](https://www.sparkfun.com/usb-logic-analyzer-24mhz-8-channel.html) &ndash; at major distributors.&thinsp; Search *"8-channel, 24MHz USB logic analyzer"* for this (and other) products.

      </div>

      Using the jumper wires, connect the analyzer to the cor&shy;res&shy;ponding (color-coded) pins on your board of choice.

      After some **PulseView** setup, you'll see the same [`AppOut`, `DbgA`, &thinsp;&hellip; `SysLed`] signals captured from *real* hardware.

  - cmds:
    text: |
      {[ht,settings]&thinsp;Configuring PulseView}

